ver stored if > 4096 chars; no truncation marker; callers have no signal the record is incomplete\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py::_run_spawn_item` (line 4355) \u2014 on non-JSON stdout from spawn.py, builds `error_msg = f\\\"spawn.py non-JSON output: {stdout_text[:200]}\\\"`. This error_msg is both stored in SQLite (via `set_item_failed`) and appended to `warnings` list returned in the tool response. Fix: remove `[:200]`. | Risk: HIGH | Why: Truncation hides the real failure cause; the operator sees only the first 200 chars of stdout exactly when full output is most needed for debugging\\n\\n### Code \u2014 Consumers\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py::dispatch_wave_status` (lines 4142\u20134208) \u2014 returns `DispatchWaveSummary` which includes `items: list[DispatchItemRecord]`, each with a `result` field. Reads directly from SQLite via `mgr.get_wave_items`. Will reflect whatever was stored \u2014 after the fix, the full result payload will be surfaced in tool responses. | Risk: LOW | Why: No code change needed; consumer already passes the field through without additional truncation. Verify the MCP tool response is not size-limited elsewhere.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py::dispatch_item_status` (lines 4074\u20134135) \u2014 the externally-callable completion path (spawned sessions call this MCP tool directly). Calls `mgr.set_item_complete(result=result)` with NO truncation (line 4099\u20134101). Not broken by this bug, but confirms the internal polling path (`_poll_until_done`) is the only site with the 4096 slice. | Risk: LOW | Why: This path is already correct; no change needed. Documents that two completion paths exist with inconsistent behavior.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/dispatch_state.py::DispatchStateManager.set_item_complete` (lines 261\u2013287) \u2014 stores `result` in SQLite `items.result TEXT` column. SQLite TEXT columns have no length constraint. The method signature accepts an unbounded `str`. No size enforcement here. | Risk: LOW | Why: No change needed; the storage layer imposes no constraint justifying the 4096 limit.\\n\\n### Code \u2014 Other References\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/models.py::DispatchItemRecord` (lines 1396\u20131415) \u2014 the Pydantic model mapping the SQLite items row to Python. The `result: str = \\\"\\\"` and `error: str = \\\"\\\"` fields impose no length constraint. | Risk: LOW | Why: No change needed; model is already compatible with unbounded strings.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/models.py::DispatchWaveSummary` (lines 1442\u20131457) \u2014 includes `items: list[DispatchItemRecord]`; the full item record including result is serialised and returned by `dispatch_wave_status`. After the fix, large results will appear here. | Risk: LOW | Why: No code change needed; serialisation is already passthrough.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/models.py::DispatchSpawnSummary` (lines 1460\u20131471) \u2014 final summary returned by `dispatch_spawn` background task. Does not include per-item result strings directly; only costs and counts. | Risk: LOW | Why: Not affected by result field size change.\\n\\n### Tests\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_dispatch_state.py::TestSetItemComplete` (lines 439\u2013512) \u2014 tests `set_item_complete` for field update, wave completion, and partial wave guard. Uses short result strings (`'{\\\"status\\\":\\\"ok\\\"}'`, `\\\"done\\\"`). No test asserts that a >4096-character result round-trips intact. | Risk: HIGH | Why: Acceptance criteria explicitly require a regression test for >4096-char completion payload. Currently missing \u2014 this gap means the bug could silently regress after the fix.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_dispatch_tools.py` (line 524 comment) \u2014 mentions `set_item_complete` in a comment about concurrent SQLite writes, but the test uses a mocked result path with a small payload. Does not exercise the truncation boundary. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: End-to-end dispatch test exists but does not cover the large-payload path through `_poll_until_done`.\\n- No test exists for the spawn non-JSON error path (`stdout_text[:200]`). The path where `spawn.py` returns non-JSON stdout is not covered. | Risk: HIGH | Why: Zero coverage for the second truncation site; no regression protection.\\n\\n### Documentation\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-milestone/SKILL.md` (line 173) \u2014 contains a bash code example that reads the result_file and prints `d.get('result','')[:200]`. This is a third truncation in AI-facing instruction code that will become inconsistent with the fix behavior. As written it teaches a [:200] pattern for result preview. | Risk: MEDIUM | Why: AI-facing instructions that model truncation patterns can propagate the anti-pattern into agent-generated dispatch scripts; content becomes stale after fix.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/ARCHITECTURE.md` (lines 369\u2013378) \u2014 documents `set_item_complete(milestone, wave_num, issue, result, cost)` signature with no mention of size constraints. Accurate post-fix; no staleness risk. | Risk: LOW | Why: No changes needed; the doc already describes the interface correctly without referencing the truncation.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/CLAUDE.md` \u2014 Dispatch Orchestration section describes `dispatch_item_status` and `dispatch_spawn` tool behavior at the documentation level. Does not reference result size. | Risk: LOW | Why: No staleness risk from fix; description remains accurate.\\n\\n### Configuration / CI\\n- `.github/workflows/code-quality.yml` (line 209) \u2014 runs `uv run -q pytest` which covers the dispatch state and dispatch tools test suites. The CI will run any new regression test added for the >4096-char round-trip. | Risk: LOW | Why: No CI config change needed; test runner already covers the affected files. New tests will automatically be picked up